WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.750
The situation in Ukraine remains quite fluid.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.039
A new government is forming.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:23.770
We don’t yet know how much legitimacy, authority
it will have over the situation.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:28.930
Violence has abated with the exception of
Crimea, but it will take a long time before

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:33.390
the dust settles and we have a good sense
as to whether a country that has really been

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:39.210
undergoing a great deal of uncertainty and
instability since the fall of the Soviet Union

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.341
is turning the corner and heading in a positive
direction.

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:48.570
There are three things to know, to keep in
mind, about the situation in Ukraine.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.840
The first is that it’s a very complicated
country politically and ethnically.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.210
Going back to the 14th century, the Polish,
the Lithuanians, the Austrians occupied large

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.930
chunks of Ukraine particularly in the west.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:07.899
And that part of the country is therefore
oriented toward Europe.

00:01:07.899 --> 00:01:13.540
People who live there tend to be ethnically
Ukrainian and speak Ukrainian as their mother

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.590
tongue.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:19.610
The eastern part of the country has tended
to be under Russian domination.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.520
There are many ethnic Russians.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:27.210
The oblast, or states in the east, have a
population that is about 40 percent ethnic

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.210
Russian.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.990
Crimea has a Russian majority -- almost 60
percent of its population is Russian.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.469
They look to Russia.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:36.020
They speak Russian.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:40.929
They identify Ukraine as a country that is
quite attached to Russia.

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:46.810
And for this reason, finding a way to keep
the country together, trying to find policies

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:51.939
to bring the country into some kind of national
unity is going to be very difficult.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:57.649
The second issue to keep an eye on is that
even though the last couple of weeks have

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:03.529
been quite remarkable -- in fact, almost a
miracle that this transition occurred as peacefully

00:02:03.529 --> 00:02:06.759
as it did -- there is much hard work ahead.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:10.429
Yanukovych did fall from power very quickly.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:15.849
The state peeled away from him, turned against
him, including his own party, the Party of

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:17.230
Regions.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:23.510
And we saw the police, the military, the bureaucracy
essentially throw their weight behind this

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.510
new government.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.300
That’s quite remarkable, and quite positive.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:33.489
But the country has a very weak institutional
infrastructure; it is on the verge of default.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:38.880
It is not yet clear when the IMF, the US and
the EU will step forward.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.019
It’s not clear whether Ukraine will be able
to make the political and economic reforms

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:49.650
necessary to get the loans to keep the country
fiscally afloat.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.790
The third point to keep an eye on is the geopolitics.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:03:00.400
Russia has always been an overlord for Ukraine,
including after the fall of the Soviet Union,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.520
but this could be the moment at which Ukraine
finally emerges from Russia’s sphere of

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.849
influence.

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:08.900
Putin has been dealt a serious blow.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.519
He picked Yanukovych.

00:03:10.519 --> 00:03:13.000
Yanukovych is now on the run.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.680
Putin wanted to build a Eurasian union with
Ukraine as Russia’s junior partner.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.900
That no longer seems possible.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.000
Ukraine has to be careful and has to balance
between ties to Europe and ties to Russia,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.709
but this really could be a moment in which
finally Ukraine enjoys more autonomy and can

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:47.250
deepen its ties to Europe and chart a course
toward rule of law and liberal democracy.

